{"name":"Frilled Shark","hex":"#939fa9","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frilled shark","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any member of two extant species of shark in genus Chlamydoselachus or their extinct close relatives,"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frilled_shark"}],"entries":[{"title":"Frilled shark","blocks":[{"text":"The frilled shark, also known as the lizard shark, is one of the two extant species of shark in the family Chlamydoselachidae. The frilled shark is considered a living fossil, because of its primitive, anguilliform (eel-like) physical traits, such as a dark-brown color, amphistyly, and a 2.0 m (6.6 ft)–long body, which has dorsal, pelvic, and anal fins located towards the tail. The common name, frilled shark, derives from the fringed appearance of the six pairs of gill slits at the shark's throat."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus1.jpg/330px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frilled_shark","wikidata":"Q194312"},{"title":"Shark","blocks":[{"text":"Sharks are a group of elasmobranch cartilaginous fishes characterized by a ribless endoskeleton, dermal denticles, five to seven gill slits on each side, and pectoral fins that are not fused to the head. Modern sharks are classified within the division Selachii and are the sister group to the Batomorphi. Some sources extend the term \"shark\" as an informal category including extinct members of Chondrichthyes with a shark-like morphology, such as hybodonts. Shark-like chondrichthyans such as Cladoselache and Doliodus first appeared in the Devonian Period, though some fossilized chondrichthyan-like scales are as old as the Late Ordovician. The earliest confirmed modern sharks (Selachii) are known from the Early Jurassic around 200 million years ago, with the oldest known member being Agaleus, though records of true sharks may extend back as far as the Permian."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark","wikidata":"Q7372"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus1.jpg/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus1.jpg/1280px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus1.jpg/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChlamydoselachus_anguineus1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_head.jpg/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_head.jpg/1280px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_head.jpg/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Closer view of the head","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChlamydoselachus_anguineus_head.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_by_garman.png/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_by_garman.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_by_garman.png/1280px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_by_garman.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_by_garman.png/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_by_garman.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the article \"An Extraordinary Shark\", the zoologist Samuel Garman depicts a frilled shark (Clamydoselachus anguineus); the superior inset depicts dorsal and ventral views of the shark's head; the inferior inset depicts two, trident-shaped teeth. (Bulletin of the Essex Institute, vol. XVI, 1884)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChlamydoselachus_anguineus_by_garman.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_NOOA.jpg/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_NOOA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_NOOA.jpg/1280px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_NOOA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_NOOA.jpg/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_NOOA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A frilled shark in its Blake Plateau habitat, in the western Atlantic Ocean (2004).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChlamydoselachus_anguineus_NOOA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_%28mouth_and_teeth%29_by_OpenCage.jpg/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_%28mouth_and_teeth%29_by_OpenCage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_%28mouth_and_teeth%29_by_OpenCage.jpg/1280px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_%28mouth_and_teeth%29_by_OpenCage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_%28mouth_and_teeth%29_by_OpenCage.jpg/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_%28mouth_and_teeth%29_by_OpenCage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The frilled shark's lower jaw has 21–29 rows of recurved, needle-like teeth, for snagging, capturing, and eating soft-bodied cephalopods, small sharks, and bony fish.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChlamydoselachus_anguineus_(mouth_and_teeth)_by_OpenCage.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Frilled_shark_throat.jpg/500px-Frilled_shark_throat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Frilled_shark_throat.jpg/1280px-Frilled_shark_throat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Frilled_shark_throat.jpg/500px-Frilled_shark_throat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The frilled shark name of the Chlamydoselachus anguineus species derives from the extended tips of the gill filaments of the shark's six pairs of gills.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrilled_shark_throat.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_teeth_-_tokyosealifepark-2019-1-8.jpg/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_teeth_-_tokyosealifepark-2019-1-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_teeth_-_tokyosealifepark-2019-1-8.jpg/500px-Chlamydoselachus_anguineus_teeth_-_tokyosealifepark-2019-1-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The jaws of the frilled shark are able to open very wide in order to engulf larger prey.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChlamydoselachus_anguineus_teeth_-_tokyosealifepark-2019-1-8.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Frilled_shark_head2.jpg/500px-Frilled_shark_head2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Frilled_shark_head2.jpg/1280px-Frilled_shark_head2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Frilled_shark_head2.jpg/500px-Frilled_shark_head2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The frilled shark is a predator with long jaws directly articulated to the cranium, at a point behind the eyes.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrilled_shark_head2.jpg"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Grey Bushchat in Sillery Gaon March 2025 by Tisha Mukherjee 01","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Grey_Bushchat_in_Sillery_Gaon_March_2025_by_Tisha_Mukherjee_01.jpg/500px-Grey_Bushchat_in_Sillery_Gaon_March_2025_by_Tisha_Mukherjee_01.jpg","ratio":1.3997488488907492,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grey_Bushchat_in_Sillery_Gaon_March_2025_by_Tisha_Mukherjee_01.jpg","share":0.493125},{"title":"Swallow Prominent on a grey wall","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Swallow_Prominent_on_a_grey_wall.jpg/500px-Swallow_Prominent_on_a_grey_wall.jpg","ratio":1,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swallow_Prominent_on_a_grey_wall.jpg","share":0.31875},{"title":"Scala di grigi","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Scala_di_grigi.jpg/500px-Scala_di_grigi.jpg","ratio":1.3333333333333333,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scala_di_grigi.jpg","share":0.305},{"title":"Madeira Grey Cat","image":"https://